[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A create builds its document in memory, so it batches like every other write

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SCRIBE-NEW-BUILDS-THE-DOCUMENT
TITLE: A create builds its document in memory, so it batches like every other write
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A create builds its document in memory and then behaves like every other
write: the mutation applies to the held graph, the checks run, the file is
rendered and parsed, and only the whole-graph reload is deferred.

Three things have to be true of a document built this way, and each is done
the way the loader does it rather than approximated.

THE GRAMMAR IS RESOLVED, NOT REBUILT. The reader leaves IMPORT_FROM_FILE as a
name with no elements; the index builder is what resolves it. That step runs
here for the one new document, through the same two lookups -- the project's
alias table, then the tree's map of parsed grammar files -- so the elements
are the one shared list every other document already points at. Note that
update_with_elements re-parents that shared list to the new grammar. It is
upstream's own call and upstream's own side effect: the loader runs it once
per document over the same list, so after any load those parents point at
whichever document was processed last, and nothing may depend on which. The
only reader of a grammar element's parent is validate_grammar_element, which
this path does not reach.

THE META IS MIRRORED, AND THE MIRROR IS UNDER CONTRACT. Every DocumentMeta
field is a join of the repository root's own basename with the document's
directory, so all of it derives from the path -- but deriving it means copying
DocumentFinder, which is the one thing this module otherwise refuses to do.
What makes that safe is a comparison rather than care: a contract builds a
document in memory, reloads the corpus for real, and asserts every meta field
identical. See [LINK: EV-SCRIBE-NEW-IN-ONE-LOAD].

THE DOCUMENT IS REGISTERED IN ALL THREE CONTAINERS a lookup goes through:
document_list, map_docs_by_paths, map_docs_by_rel_paths. No index surgery is
needed beyond that, because get_node_by_uid_weak WALKS document_list rather
than reading the graph database -- which is why a node created in a batch is
already a valid relation target for the next write in the same batch, and why
a second create cannot reuse its UID.

ONE RULE HAD TO BE STATED THAT USED TO HOLD BY ACCIDENT. A document written
where DocumentFinder does not look -- under a directory named output, or
outside the project's include masks -- reaches the disk and is then simply
absent after the next reload. The shape this replaces caught that without
meaning to, by looking the new document up in a reloaded tree and failing to
find it. A create now refuses such a destination outright, before anything is
built.
<<<
